+
+## Container budgets
+
+With `SPINEL_WORKERS` unset or empty, Linux reads the process cgroup from
+`/proc/self/cgroup` and resolves its mount through `/proc/self/mountinfo`.
+The smallest visible ancestor quota wins: v2 `cpu.max`, then v1
+`cpu.cfs_quota_us` / `cpu.cfs_period_us`. Unlimited or invalid quotas fall
+back to `sched_getaffinity`, then `sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN)`.
+Workers are `max(1, min(online CPUs, affinity CPUs, ceil(quota / period)))`;
+missing bounds are omitted. Main worker 0 is included, so a two-CPU container
+gets at most two execution workers. Monitor and GC sweeper threads are
+separate from execution workers. The compile-time slot bound still applies.
+
+The general default uses factor 1. A blocking-I/O application can set
+`SPINEL_WORKER_FACTOR=2` before its first thread to use
+`ceil(2 * quota / period)`, still bounded by affinity and online CPUs. Scaling
+happens before rounding: 1.5 CPUs become 3 workers. Without a finite quota
+the factor has no effect. There is no image-specific worker count.
+An explicit `SPINEL_WORKERS` still overrides detection, including values above
+the CPU budget. The count is frozen at the first `Thread.new` or resource query.
+Limits hidden above the cgroup namespace's mount root cannot be inspected.
+Escaped mount paths fall back to affinity; non-Linux builds use online CPUs.
+
+The same cgroup lookup reads the smallest visible `memory.max` (v2), or
+`memory.limit_in_bytes` (v1). Unlimited limits preserve existing GC pacing.
+For finite memory M, the object and old-string collection triggers are capped
+at M/8 each; the young-string trigger is capped at M/(8 * execution workers).
+This ceiling applies to initial floors, explicit GC floors, and adaptive retunes.
+The existing 256 KiB floors and live-set heuristics remain below the ceiling.
+These are collection triggers, not hard heap or RSS caps: live objects, external
+allocations, stacks and socket buffers still consume memory. Kernel limits
+remain authoritative. Budgets are discovered at startup, not polled for changes.
+
+`make resource-test` tests fake proc and cgroup trees through the production
+parser, without a runtime environment seam, root privileges, or Docker.
